DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261124T163000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261124T180000
SUMMARY:“It’s lucky that we live in Iceland”: Nordic exceptionalism a
 nd narratives of climate in/vulnerability in contemporary Iceland
LOCATION:Scott Polar Research Institute\, Lecture Theatre
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273869/
CATEGORIES:Politics,International,Policy
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Utsa Mukherjee (Education\, Brunel University of Londo
 n)\nScott Polar Research Institute - HCEP (Histories\, Cultures\, Environm
 ents and Politics) Research Seminars\nTopics: Politics\, International\, P
 olicy\nDetails: https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273869/\nWhile climate 
 vulnerability\, that is the geographical distribution of risks on account 
 of the climate crisis\, is widely discussed\; relatively little scholarly 
 attention has been paid to how colonial fantasies of climate invulnerabili
 ty underpin climate inaction and justify the maintenance of status quo (We
 atherill\, 2025). This paper contributes to the emergent body of critical 
 scholarship on the politics of climate in/vulnerability through a focus on
  contemporary Iceland. Drawing on in-depth interviews with more than 80 pa
 rticipants from across Iceland\, who recognise the reality of anthropogeni
 c climate change and are committed to climate action\, we show how Iceland
 ’s geographical location is invoked by participants to advance ideas of 
 relative climate in/vulnerability of the island. Analysed through prisms o
 f critical feminist and decolonial politics\, these narratives assume two 
 main fo
END:VEVENT
